    itemsPurchasedDescription: 'Ice cream, doughnuts, and cupcakes.',
    narrativeReview: "We approached the Butterbeer Counter and were welcomed by the staff member, who asked what we would like to order. I ordered an ice cream. The staff member then asked whether I would like anything else, and I ordered the doughnuts and the cupcakes. I also asked whether they had any gluten-free options. The staff member explained that the gluten-free options available were the Butterbeer meringue, the Butterbeer fudge and the Butterbeer drink. They then asked whether I had any allergies, and I confirmed that I did not. Once the order was prepared, I was handed the ice cream separately in my hand, while the remaining items were provided in individual open paper trays. This created an immediate practical issue, as there was no tray or suitable way to carry all of the items together. With the ice cream occupying one hand, I was only able to carry one or two of the remaining items at a time with the other. As a result, I had to leave some of the items behind, walk to a table, return to collect the remaining items and repeat the process whilst still holding the ice cream. This made collecting the order unnecessarily difficult. We asked whether a tray was available, and the staff member explained that they did not have any. However, I could see several dirty trays piled up nearby, which suggested that trays were used within the area but were not available for visitors at that time. When looking for a table, I found that many of them had not been cleaned. I initially placed some of the items on a table to the side whilst waiting for another table to become available. Even the seating area contained crumbs, indicating that tables and seating were not being cleared promptly between visitors. The presentation of the ice cream was inconsistent. It had been placed onto the cone at an angle and appeared noticeably squashed, leaning to one side as though it could fall off. Although it remained intact, it did not look as carefully prepared as those served to other visitors. I observed that other visitors' ice creams were taller, more evenly shaped and better presented. In terms of quality, the ice cream itself was acceptable but not exceptional. I found it slightly too sweet, and whilst the cone was of an average standard, I felt the offering could have been enhanced through more premium options, such as chocolate-dipped cones or other customisation choices that would better reflect the premium nature of the Studio Tour experience. The cupcakes were considerably smaller than they initially appeared. Much of their height came from the buttercream topping, while the sponge itself was very small and largely hidden within the paper wrapping. This gave the impression that the product was larger than it actually was. The doughnut was of particularly poor quality. It was extremely stale, which significantly affected the eating experience and suggested that it was not fresh. Overall, the ordering process was straightforward, and the staff members answered my questions appropriately. However, the lack of trays, the condition of the seating area and tables, the inconsistent presentation of the ice cream, the size of the cupcakes, the stale doughnut and the way the badge activity was administered detracted from the overall experience.",
